WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.680
Even though James Bond films are espionage action&nbsp;
flicks, they also contain a fair amount of humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.120
to add a dash of comedic fun and to soften the&nbsp;
blow of some rather violent scenes—especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.400
death scenes. From the hilarious to the macabre,&nbsp;
here are Bond's best post-kill one-liners.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.720
In “Tomorrow Never Dies,” Pierce Brosnan drops an&nbsp;
absolutely fantastic one-liner that cracks us up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.840
right after a particularly nasty kill. At this&nbsp;
point in the film, Bond is in a massive facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.720
that contains a printing press that looks&nbsp;
like it generates 100 newspapers per second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.480
Reams of paper are weaving in and out of a complex&nbsp;
system of machinery, and the sound is deafening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.960
And it's here that Brosnan goes toe to toe with&nbsp;
a henchman, and you can probably guess where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.600
bad guy ends up by the fight's end. That's&nbsp;
right — directly into the printing press.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.680
As with so many of our deaths listed&nbsp;
here, the sight is pretty gruesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.000
The body jams up the printing press, and the&nbsp;
newspaper flying through the machinery is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.200
stained red from the victim's blood. Bond looks at&nbsp;
a pile of newspapers hot off the press and says,

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:02.960
"They'll print anything these days."

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.800
Including a criminal henchman — literally.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.040
In Sean Connery's very first 007 film,&nbsp;
“Dr. No,” we find Bond innocently driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.440
his car on a winding dirt road when,&nbsp;
suddenly, he notices he's being followed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.240
The car behind Bond is aggressively chasing&nbsp;
him, even tapping his rear bumper a few times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.480
Bond tries to lose these bad guys by&nbsp;
driving extremely fast around turns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.560
hoping the villain will fail to keep up at&nbsp;
those speeds. It's quite a dangerous game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.960
to play since one side of the road is a cliff&nbsp;
that looks at least a couple of stories high.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.360
Bond fails to lose them at first, but to his luck,&nbsp;
there's a construction vehicle in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.000
the road doing who knows what. Conveniently,&nbsp;
Bond's car is small enough to drive through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.800
a small opening, but the henchmen in the car&nbsp;
behind Bond can't make it. At the last second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.120
the villains try grinding to a halt, but they&nbsp;
drive off the edge, and their car rolls down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.080
the mountain, exploding in the process. As a&nbsp;
clueless bystander, the construction worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.120
is completely bewildered, wondering what&nbsp;
happened. Ever ready with a quip, Bond says,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.800
"I think they were on their way to a funeral."

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.760
Honestly, it’s pretty brilliant. After&nbsp;
all, the construction worker probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.880
took it to mean that the henchmen&nbsp;
were in a rush to get to a funeral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.240
but Bond was implying the henchmen were&nbsp;
on their way to their own funerals.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.680
This one-liner comes from the fan-favorite&nbsp;
film “Goldfinger.” In the pre-credit scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.160
Bond accomplishes his mission by blowing up a&nbsp;
drug laboratory. He's then warned by an ally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.680
that the drug lords will seek revenge&nbsp;
and that he should leave immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.480
but that just isn't the Bond way. Before the spy&nbsp;
hops on a plane home, he must first sleep with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.040
woman that he's been courting, but little does&nbsp;
Bond know that she plans to kill him. She hides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.160
a hitman in her apartment, welcomes Bond in, and&nbsp;
then does something very clever. She casually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.960
mentions that Bond's gun makes her uncomfortable,&nbsp;
prompting the agent to take it off his person.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.600
She kisses Bond while the henchman sneaks&nbsp;
up, but 007 catches the reflection of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.240
would-be attacker in her eye. No, really, he does.&nbsp;
That’s how eyes work. He dodges the first attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.880
but from that point on, the two engage in a&nbsp;
rather equal wrestling match in the hotel room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.360
It culminates with Bond throwing the henchman&nbsp;
into a full bathtub, and while the bad guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.400
gets a hold of Bond's gun, 007 thinks fast&nbsp;
and smacks an electric fan into the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.920
electrocuting the villain to&nbsp;
death. That's when Bond says,

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:18.480
"Shocking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.720
Positively shocking."

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.960
But was he saying these words about the&nbsp;
woman who betrayed him? Or was he saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.720
them about how his victim perished?&nbsp;
From his glances, it looks like both.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.000
To fully appreciate this one-liner, you have to&nbsp;
understand something about the main villain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.120
“From Russia with Love.” The major baddie&nbsp;
in this 1963 action flick is Rosa Klebb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.240
a former Russian counterintelligence higher-up&nbsp;
who joins the mysterious organization SPECTRE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.520
as "No. 3." While Klebb has similarities with&nbsp;
other Bond villains, she does have a rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.120
unique weapon — shoes that conceal poison-tipped&nbsp;
blades. When you least expect it, Klebb ejects one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.720
of these blades from her shoes and starts kicking&nbsp;
at you. A single cut can land you in the hospital.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.840
At the end of the film, Bond is relaxing&nbsp;
in a hotel with Bond girl Tatiana Romanova.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.400
Meanwhile, Klebb, disguised as a maid, is&nbsp;
trying one last time to eliminate Bond and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.880
retrieve a valuable device known as the Lektor.&nbsp;
Fortunately, Bond and Romanova win a fight with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.200
Klebb in their hotel room, which ends with&nbsp;
Romanova shooting Klebb dead. Afterward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.711
Romanova remarks that she was a, quote,&nbsp;
“horrible woman." Bond agrees, saying,

00:04:28.711 --> 00:04:29.920
"Yes, she's had her kicks"

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.280
It’s a clever little jab at&nbsp;
her killer sense of fashion.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.280
James Bond delivered this little&nbsp;
quip after defeating one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.440
difficult baddies in the entire&nbsp;
007 franchise — Oddjob. Hailing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.680
from the movie “Goldfinger,” Oddjob is a&nbsp;
short but extremely formidable opponent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.400
working for the main villain, Auric Goldfinger.&nbsp;
At this point in the film, Oddjob and Bond are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.440
fighting each other inside Fort Knox, and it's&nbsp;
epic. Bond hurls a heavy gold block at Oddjob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.920
but it just bounces off his chest. Then Bond&nbsp;
tries jumping on Oddjob and tackling him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.760
but Oddjob picks up 007 and tosses him like he's&nbsp;
a frisbee. The spy then attempts punching his foe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.080
whacking him with a stick, and throwing&nbsp;
a metal-ringed hat — all of which fail.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.720
Clearly, Bond has no choice but to think outside&nbsp;
of the box — and that's exactly what he does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.760
At one point, Oddjob goes to retrieve his metal&nbsp;
hat, which is lodged between metal bars. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.360
same moment Oddjob touches his bowler, Bond puts&nbsp;
an open wire onto the bars, electrocuting Oddjob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.120
to death. Later on, CIA agent Felix Leiter asks&nbsp;
where his butler friend was and Bond replies,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:32.880
"He blew a fuse."

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.800
Quite literally, we might add.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.880
It should go without saying, but one of the major&nbsp;
elements of “Goldfinger” is gold. The main villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.360
is named Auric Goldfinger, with the first two&nbsp;
letters of his first name representing gold on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.640
periodic table. At one point, the bad guy kills&nbsp;
a woman and paints her whole body in gold because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.040
why not? And, of course, Goldfinger loves&nbsp;
gold so much that he dedicates his entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.840
criminal career to smuggling it and even&nbsp;
gathers a small militia to rob Fort Knox.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.880
As a result, it's only fitting that Goldfinger&nbsp;
would have a "golden" end. During the climax of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.600
the film, Bond fights Goldfinger on a plane,&nbsp;
which culminates in Goldfinger getting sucked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.720
out a window. A little bit later, Pussy Galore —&nbsp;
yes, that’s her name — asks Bond what happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.160
Goldfinger. Bond spills the news that Goldfinger&nbsp;
is dead, but he does so with the clever one-liner,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.600
"Playing his golden harp."

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.680
Yeah, we've got to admit, it's a golden line.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.480
In “Thunderball,” our suave spy gets one of the&nbsp;
smoothest kills in 007 history. Bond is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.680
up against Emilio Largo, a villain who stole&nbsp;
nuclear weapons and is threatening to use them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.080
To help locate them, Bond is attempting&nbsp;
to recruit Domino, who's Largo's mistress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:44.160
Bond and Domino have a rather heartfelt&nbsp;
and serious conversation on a beach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.120
where Bond explains to Domino that Largo&nbsp;
killed her brother and may soon kill millions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.960
After she agrees to help, Bond begins&nbsp;
instructing her on how to use some gadgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.440
to get the job done, but that's when one of&nbsp;
Largo's henchmen starts sneaking up on them.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.400
Domino, cool as a cucumber, casually tells Bond&nbsp;
that the henchman is right behind him, and Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.440
equally as cool, says, quote, "Really?" In the&nbsp;
flash of a second, Bond picks up a speargun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.360
turns around, and shoots the henchman.&nbsp;
With the kill accomplished, Bond remarks,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.440
"I think he got the point."

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.840
That's what you get for trying&nbsp;
to sneak up on James Bond.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.880
While James Bond's post-kill one-liners&nbsp;
are generally one and done, this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.680
comment has been used in two separate films.&nbsp;
In “You Only Live Twice,” the evil Blofeld has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.520
an elaborate volcano lair equipped with a pool of&nbsp;
flesh-eating piranhas that he uses for executions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.560
At one point, Blofeld is scolding an underling&nbsp;
named Helga Brandt for failing to kill Bond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.320
As punishment, Blofeld mercilessly&nbsp;
activates a trap door that drops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.840
Brandt into the piranha tank.&nbsp;
She's eaten alive in seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.320
which is Blofeld's way of showing everyone&nbsp;
watching that he doesn't tolerate failure.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.760
Anyway, the moral of the story is you&nbsp;
don't want to fall into this water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.720
Well, later on, Bond gets into a brawl with&nbsp;
Hans, one of Blofeld's strongest henchmen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.520
After going back and forth a bit, Bond finally&nbsp;
manages to throw Hans into the pool of piranhas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.760
which promptly ends his life. Bond&nbsp;
encouraged the fish to eat, saying simply,

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.440
"Bon appetit."

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.920
But this quip also appears in “License to&nbsp;
Kill,” when Bond breaks into an aquarium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.520
at night to locate hidden drugs. He discovers an&nbsp;
incubator full of maggots, which are fed to fish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.960
Right then, a henchman finds&nbsp;
him, but Bond uses some kung fu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.320
to throw the baddie into the bed of&nbsp;
maggots, and then shuts the drawer.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:25.840
"Bon appetit."

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.440
Disgusting.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.480
This one-liner is a bit more gruesome for&nbsp;
Bond films than we're typically used to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.120
It comes from “On Her Majesty's Secret&nbsp;
Service,” delivered by one George Lazenby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.360
Leading up to the line, Bond is skiing&nbsp;
down a mountain with Teresa "Tracy" Draco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.480
and chasing after them is a group of henchmen who&nbsp;
are also on skis. It's quite an exciting sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:50.880
involving bad guys using machine guns,&nbsp;
an epic ski chase, and an avalanche.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.600
But the craziest part of the chase is when&nbsp;
everyone skis past this huge snow-blowing machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.120
One sorry henchman falls into the&nbsp;
contraption, which has massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.920
sharp gears that eat up snow before shooting it&nbsp;
out. After getting caught in that monstrosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:09.440
the snow blower shoots out red snow. Yikes. And&nbsp;
that's when Bond yells over to Tracy, saying,

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.720
"He had lots of guts."

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:16.080
It works both figuratively, in that the henchman&nbsp;
was brave enough to chase after James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.040
and also quite literally. Not&nbsp;
that we need to explain that.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.640
Ever see an injury on-screen so painful that you&nbsp;
feel it even as a viewer? That's what you'll feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:29.280
watching this death sequence. In “Diamonds Are&nbsp;
Forever,” a pair of assassins named Mr. Wint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.960
and Mr. Kidd are hunting Bond in the service of&nbsp;
Blofeld. The evil duo find our hero on a boat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:40.000
and they attempt to kill him by bringing a small&nbsp;
bomb aboard. Bond quickly dispenses with Mr. Kidd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.960
but it's the second villain&nbsp;
we're interested in — Mr. Wint.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.880
As Mr. Wint and Bond fight back and&nbsp;
forth, 007 gets the upper hand by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.120
yanking Mr. Wint's arms through his legs&nbsp;
and upwards. It's obviously painful given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.120
the sound he makes. While Bond has him&nbsp;
in this terribly uncomfortable position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:00.960
he attaches the bomb to Mr. Wint, then throws him&nbsp;
overboard. After the bad guy explodes, Bond says,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.280
"Well, he certainly left with&nbsp;
his tails between his legs."

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.320
Yeah, 007 would never let&nbsp;
a bad guy die with dignity.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:12.240
“Live and Let Die” might just feature the most&nbsp;
creative death in the entire James Bond franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.840
and that's really saying something.&nbsp;
The bizarre kill happens at the climax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.120
when the main villain, Dr. Kananga, captures&nbsp;
007 and the new Bond girl, Solitaire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:25.360
Like all Bond villains, Dr. Kananga&nbsp;
must execute Bond in an elaborate way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.520
which naturally gives Bond the opportunity&nbsp;
to escape. This time, the villain will try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.720
to kill Bond and his girl by slowly lowering&nbsp;
them into a pool of sharks. But 007 escapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.520
by using his gadget watch to saw through the&nbsp;
rope, and then he starts fighting Kananga.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:44.080
The battle becomes a lot more suspenseful when&nbsp;
the two fall into the shark-infested water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:47.680
but it may surprise you to know that the&nbsp;
sharks never kill either one of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:51.040
Instead, while underwater, Bond&nbsp;
takes a gas-compressed pellet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:57.200
and forces Dr. Kananga to swallow it. The result?&nbsp;
Dr. Kananga inflates to several times his size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:02.880
floats to the ceiling, and explodes. Talk&nbsp;
about a wild death. Bond's only remark is:

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.040
"He always did have an&nbsp;
inflated opinion of himself."

